The proof that this project is "run on quantum" lies in its direct integration with Qiskit (IBM's Quantum Computing SDK) and the way it handles cryptography at the physical level of quantum mechanics.

1. Quantum Circuit Generation
In 
key_generator.py
, the system doesn't generate "random" numbers using standard math functions. Instead, it literally constructs a Quantum Circuit:

python
qc = QuantumCircuit(current_chunk_size, current_chunk_size)
for i in range(current_chunk_size):
    qc.h(i) # This is a HADAMARD GATE
qc.measure(range(current_chunk_size), range(current_chunk_size))
The Hadamard Gate ($H$) is the fundamental gate that puts a qubit into a state of superposition (being both 0 and 1 at the same time). When the system calls qc.measure, it forces the qubit to collapse into a definite state. The resulting 0 or 1 is truly random according to the laws of quantum physics.

2. BB84 Protocol Implementation
The project implements the 
BB84 Protocol
, which is the world's most famous Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) algorithm. It uses different "bases" (rectilinear and diagonal) to transmit information securely.

3. IBM Quantum Backend Integration
The service is designed to run on two types of quantum environments:

Local Simulator: Uses AerSimulator (high-performance local quantum simulation).
IBM Quantum Hardware: If you provide an IBM_QUANTUM_API_KEY in your .env, the code connects to actual IBM Quantum processors (like ibm_osaka) via the QiskitRuntimeService.
